#--: Overview

The #~"net" unit provides TCP sockets on localhost plus readiness
polling: the transport for tool daemons such as #~"zls --listen".
The v1 surface is deliberately small: a loopback listener,
#~"accept" / #~"connect" producing bidirectional connections that
satisfy the #~"io" #~"Reader" / #~"Writer" protocols, and
poll-based readiness with a millisecond timeout.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"net.listen ...",
#~"net.connect ..."; there is no unqualified re-export.

#b:"Operational notes." Connections and listeners close
#b:"explicitly" (there is no RAII yet): call #~".close" when
done. Double closes are safe no-ops, so a cleanup path may close
unconditionally. Every fallible member returns
#~"(Result ... E: IoError)" -- the errno domain is shared with
#~"io", since socket I/O interleaves with file I/O.

#b:"Byte counts and the #~\"(Box T)\" arm rule." A union arm
holds reftypes, so a valtype success value rides in a #~"Box"
type argument -- which collapses onto the payload cell. The
byte-count members #~"read" / #~"write" are the stdlib's worked
example: they return #~"(Result (Box u64) E: IoError)", the
native side supplies the bare count, and a match arm narrows
straight to the #~"u64" element -- no #~"Box" ever appears in
user code:

#code language=zerolang: """
  buf: Bytes
  nr: c.read into: buf max: 4096
  match (nr)
  case ok  then print "read \{nr} bytes"   # nr narrows to the bare u64
  case err then io.eprintln "read failed"
"""


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Types"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=listener: #b:"Listener" -- #~"class { fd: i32  closed: bool } as { accept  close }"]
  [#*: #> to=conn: #b:"Conn" -- #~"class { fd: i32  closed: bool } as { :Reader  :Writer  read  write  flush  close }"]

#p: #b:"Functions"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=listen: #b:"net.listen" -- #~"function {port: u16} out (Result Listener E: IoError)"]
  [#*: #> to=connect: #b:"net.connect" -- #~"function {port: u16} out (Result Conn E: IoError)"]
  [#*: #> to=poll-readable: #b:"net.pollReadable" -- #~"function {fd: i32 timeoutMillis: i32} out (Result (Box bool) E: IoError)"]


#--: listener

#code language=zerolang: """
  Listener: class {
      fd: i32
      closed: bool
  } as {
      accept: function {:this} out (Result Conn E: IoError) is native
      close:  function {:this} out (Result null E: IoError) is native
  }
"""

A bound, listening TCP socket on 127.0.0.1, produced by
#> to=listen: #~"net.listen". #~"accept" blocks until a client
connects and returns the #~"Conn" for that client. #~"close"
releases the socket; closing an already-closed listener is a
safe no-op.

The #~"fd" field is the raw file descriptor -- usable with
#> to=poll-readable: #~"net.pollReadable" to wait for activity
without blocking in #~"accept".


#--: conn

#code language=zerolang: """
  Conn: class {
      fd: i32
      closed: bool
  } as {
      :Reader
      :Writer
      read:  function {:this into: Bytes max: u64} out (Result (Box u64) E: IoError) is native
      write: function {:this from: ByteView} out (Result (Box u64) E: IoError) is native
      flush: function {:this} out (Result null E: IoError) is native
      close: function {:this} out (Result null E: IoError) is native
  }
"""

One bidirectional TCP connection, produced by #~"Listener.accept"
(server side) or #> to=connect: #~"net.connect" (client side).
#~"Conn" declares #~":Reader" and #~":Writer" conformance, so it
plugs into any API written against the #~"io" stream protocols.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"read into: buf max: n" -- appends up to #~"max" bytes
    onto #~"buf" and returns the count actually read. A count of
    #b:"0" means the peer closed its end]
  [#*: #~"write from: bv" -- writes the view's bytes, looping
    until every byte is out; the returned count equals the input
    length on success]
  [#*: #~"flush" -- completes the #~"Writer" conformance. Socket
    writes go through directly, so nothing is buffered here and
    the call always succeeds]
  [#*: #~"close" -- shuts the connection down; double closes are
    safe no-ops]

#code language=zerolang: """
  msg: Bytes
  msg.append from: 104.u8   # 'h'
  msg.append from: 105.u8   # 'i'
  bv: ByteView.borrow from: msg.listView
  wr: c.write from: bv
  match (wr)
  case ok  then print "wrote \{wr} bytes"
  case err then io.eprintln "write failed"
"""


#--: listen

#code language=zerolang: """
  listen: function {port: u16} out (Result Listener E: IoError) is native
"""

Bind + listen on #~"127.0.0.1:<port>" (#~"SO_REUSEADDR", backlog
16). The returned #~"Listener" accepts client connections; errors
(port in use, permission denied) surface as #~"IoError".

#code language=zerolang: """
  lr: net.listen port: 8437
  match (lr)
  case ok then {
      ar: lr.accept
      match (ar)
      case ok  then print "client connected"
      case err then io.eprintln "accept failed"
  }
  case err then io.eprintln "listen failed"
"""


#--: connect

#code language=zerolang: """
  connect: function {port: u16} out (Result Conn E: IoError) is native
"""

Open a client connection to #~"127.0.0.1:<port>". The returned
#~"Conn" is ready for #~"read" / #~"write"; a refused or
unreachable port surfaces as #~"IoError".


#--: poll_readable

#code language=zerolang: """
  pollReadable: function {fd: i32 timeoutMillis: i32} out (Result (Box bool) E: IoError) is native
"""

True when #~"fd" has readable data (or a hangup to drain) within
#~"timeoutMillis"; #~"0" polls without blocking. Works on sockets
and standard input (fd 0), which is how a daemon can multiplex a
TCP transport with stdio. An interrupted wait reports
not-readable rather than an error.

The result is the second #~"(Box T)" collapse in this unit: the
match arm binds the bare #~"bool".

#code language=zerolang: """
  pr: net.pollReadable fd: c.fd timeoutMillis: 100
  match (pr)
  case ok  then { if pr then print "data waiting" }
  case err then io.eprintln "poll failed"
"""
